[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Missing unit test cases assert several contract postconditions and invariants that remain unverified: the critical-path postcondition for a graph containing no nodes, where the returned sequence is empty and the cumulative weight zero; the scheduling invariant that nodes registered after scheduling has begun are classified READY when every upstream prerequisite is already SUCCEEDED so they remain eligible for dispatch; the promotion postcondition that only dependents still PENDING transition to READY while dependents already advanced retain their state; the SKIP_DOWNSTREAM postcondition that every transitive dependent becomes SKIPPED exactly once; the cancellation postconditions that setting cancellation status marks all non-terminal nodes CANCELLED and that a run begun after cancellation is aborted with DAGCancelledError; and the report postcondition that a run applying CONTINUE_ON_ERROR completes with dependents blocked by an upstream failure reported among skipped nodes while unblocked independent nodes still succeed.
[BLAME] benchmark_1/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: The lifecycle-registration change recorded in the CHANGE header resolved singleton availability but left several statement families without any caller-thread observation: the node-action wrapper, per-node preemption check, retry/backoff loop, per-node timeout elevation, success/failure result construction and scheduler transitions, cancellation result paths, and result recording all execute exclusively inside the concurrency executor's worker threads, while the defensive collaborator-resolution fallbacks taken when singleton resolution raises and the empty-wave guard remain unreachable for any contracted caller. Because the concurrency postcondition guarantees worker-thread dispatch, these spans cannot be observed from the calling thread, so restructure the bookkeeping so it executes on the caller thread, or annotate each such span with # pragma: no cover (assumption: <reason>) stating the guarantee (worker-thread dispatch, or the registered-singleton and non-empty-wave preconditions).
